  "walkthroughs": [
    {
      "tag": "Stop 1",
      "h2": "Piaget's four stages",
      "body_html": "<p class=\"big\"><span class=\"hl p\">Jean Piaget</span> said children think differently at different ages.</p><ul><li>0-2: <b>Sensorimotor</b> — touch, taste, grab.</li><li>2-7: <b>Preoperational</b> — pretend play, words.</li><li>7-11: <b>Concrete operational</b> — real-world logic.</li><li>11+: <b>Formal operational</b> — abstract ideas.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stop 2",
      "h2": "Babies and toddlers",
      "body_html": "<p>Babies learn through senses and movement.</p><p>At about 8 months they realise things <b>still exist when hidden</b> — 'object permanence'. Peek-a-boo time. 👶</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stop 3",
      "h2": "Teens think bigger",
      "body_html": "<p>By 11+, you can think about ideas that aren't there — fairness, the future, what-ifs.</p><p>That's why teen years are full of big questions: 'Why?', 'What if?', 'Is that fair?' 🤔</p>"
    }
  ],
